Title = {Contribution of the former Hygiene Laboratory to
   tetrachlorodibenzodioxin (TCDD) assessment in the environment after the
   1976 ICMESA accident},
Booktitle = {CHEMISTRY, MAN AND ENVIRONMENT: THE SEVESO ACCIDENT 20 YEARS ON -
   MONITORING, EPIDEMIOLOGY AND REMEDIATION},
Year = {1999},
Pages = {17-28},
Note = {Meeting on Chemistry, Man and Environment, MILAN, ITALY, OCT 21-22, 1996},
Organization = {Fdn Lombardia Ambiente},
Abstract = {On 10 July 1976 a toxic mixture was generated from the ICMESA
   trichlorophenol production plant due to the failure of the safety valve,
   the reaction mixture containing high quantities of
   2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin was released into the surrounding
   areas.
   The Provincial Laboratory for Hygiene and Prophylactics, together with
   the ``Mario Negri{''} Institute in Milan, and the Superior Institute of
   Health Service in Rome, were charged to set up and carry out the
   analytical surveys on samples of different kinds, in order to define the
   contaminated area and, subsequently, to verify the effectiveness of the
   decontamination methods.
   After that important experience, the Micropollutants Laboratory was
   established for the purpose of avoiding the dissipation of the acquired
   knowledge. Its activity concerned two fields in particular-the
   atmospheric pollution from nonconventional pollutants and the
   measurement of dioxins in different matrixes generated in various
   industrial activities.
   Insofar as the latter field is concerned, analyses of public and
   industrial waste incinerators emissions from 1990, and foundry plants
   emissions from 1992, have become routine.
   These ordinary problems have been supported by other sporadic
   activities, such as measurement of PCDDs and PCDFs in both soil and
   waste from unauthorized dumps and waste industrial areas, analysis of
   compost before and during fermentation, evaluation of PCDDs and PCDFs
   concentrations in urban ambient air, setting up or testing of new
   extraction and purification methods, specific studies for the detection
   of dioxin released from waste characterized by high concentrations of
   PCDDs and PCDFs.},
