   DECENTRALIZED AND CENTRALIZED AC SYSTEMS IN RESIDENTIAL BUILDINGS BY
   COMMISSIONING},
Booktitle = {FIFTH INTERNATIONAL WORKSHOP ON ENERGY AND ENVIRONMENT OF RESIDENTIAL
   BUILDINGS AND THIRD INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON BUILT ENVIRONMENT AND
   PUBLIC HEALTH, VOL I AND II, PROCEEDINGS},
Year = {2009},
Pages = {1768-1775},
Note = {5th International Workshop on Energy and Environment of Residential
   Buildings/3rd International Conference on Built Environment and Public
   Health, Guilin, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 29-31, 2009},
Organization = {Hunan Univ; Univ Hong Kong; Tsinghua Univ},
Abstract = {Urban building energy consumption is increasing rapidly nowadays in
   China. The amount of this energy consumption is also vast and going
   burdensome. Accompanying with this vast energy consumption, urban
   thermal environment is getting worse. With the premise of supplying
   comfortable and healthy environments in buildings, minimization of
   energy consumption of building energy systems is the important and
   urgent objective of mankind. At present, the decentralized split-type
   air conditioners commonly used in urban residential buildings consume
   large part of building energy supplying. Recent years, in some new
   residential buildings, centralized or semi-centralized fan-coil air
   conditioning systems are preferred and recommended by some real estate
   suppliers and some house owners. Which of the two is more reasonable and
   energy efficient should be analyzed before their implementation. In this
   paper, the systematic characteristics of both decentralized split-type
   air conditioner system and semi-centralized fan-coil air conditioning
   system(in this paper, the semi-centralized fan-coil air conditioning
   system is referred as centralized AC system for abbreviation) are
   simulated based on dynamic energy consumption analysis software
   Energy-plus under the condition of different load ratio (some occupants
   are absent and their air conditionings are switched off), the systematic
   efficiency and energy consumption characteristics of both kind of system
   are compared with each other and also compared with the simulation
   values obtained by Energy-plus simulation software. Within the
   comparison of systematic energy consumption characteristics between a
   real system and the simulation results of Energy-plus, a new concept
   ``index of systematic deviation{''} is proposed in this paper. With this
   concept and corresponding approaches, AC system can be designed,
   diagnosed, retrofitted and optimized in a correct and reasonable way
   (this kind of process can be also called Commissioning)(ASHRAE, 1996).},
