   D.C., MAY, 1997},
Organization = {Amer Psychol Soc},
Abstract = {Elder (n = 106) and young (n = 141) adults' perceptions of a
   ``patient's{''} decisions regarding continuation or termination of
   medical treatment are examined, with emphasis on attitudes toward
   treatment withholding vs. withdrawal from treatment. Withdrawal from
   treatment is seen as similar to a self-administered lethal injection.
   These actions are viewed as suicidal and highly unconventional, and the
   patient is seen as less competent than when death followed either CPR
   failure or CPR withholding. Results are examined in light of competing
   theoretical explanations and recent evidence regarding elder adults' end
   of life decisions.},
