   07-09, 2017},
Organization = {Malaysian Tech Scientist Assoc},
Abstract = {Poor performance, low engagement, negative attitudes, and technological
   divide between teachers and students are identified as culprits in
   identifying problems associated with science education. Nevertheless,
   teachers have always adapted with the significant changes in the
   environment. The paper investigated the learners' performance, attitude,
   engagement and understanding towards learning physics under a flipped
   classroom. The study used the Mixed Methods approach that involved two
   parts: the quantitative and qualitative. A total of 37 students were
   considered as participants in the study that were subjected to the
   8-week long Flipped Classroom environment. Questionnaires on attitude
   towards learning physics and student engagement were distributed to and
   answered by the participants before and after the intervention. For the
   students' perceptions, weekly reflections were collected, analyzed, and
   recorded. In addition, focus group discussion was also conducted to
   further validate the results of the intervention. Results of the study
   reveal that overall, their performance in quizzes and problem-solving
   have been ``Very Good.{''} The difference between mean scores of student
   engagement proved to be significant before and after the flipped
   classroom intervention. There also exists a significant difference
   between the mean scores of students' attitude towards learning physics
   before and after intervention. There were positive views that were
   elicited from the students.},
